+      recipient_address: "Ghent, Belgium"
+      date: "July 2026"
+      title: "Application - Engine Programmer"
+      opening: "Dear Hiring Team,"
+      closing: "Kind regards,"
+      body: |
+        I'm applying for the Engine Programmer role. I'll be direct about where I'm coming from: I'm
+        a C++ systems engineer who spends his research career writing performant, multithreaded,
+        GPU-accelerated code, and who builds engines and runtimes for the love of it. In my own
+        time I designed and maintain Cardinal, a C++20 virtual machine with its own bytecode
+        compiler, fiber-based concurrency and an embedding API, the kind of low-level, performance-
+        and memory-sensitive work an engine lives on. I want to do that full-time, on real games.
+
+        The games matter to me as a player, Baldur's Gate 3 and Divinity: Original Sin 2 among
+        them, and game development is where I started: my team took second place at the Belgian
+        Game Development Championship for a room-scale game built in Unity with motion-tracked
+        physical controllers, and my earliest engineering work was real-time C++ against OptiTrack
+        motion capture. I trained as a computer-science engineer with a compiler and
+        programming-languages focus (ACM SIGPLAN and ICFP research-competition medals), so bytecode,
+        runtimes, data-driven systems and profiling are native territory, not a stretch.
+
+        I'll be honest about the gaps too: I haven't shipped a AAA title, and my hands-on time with
+        modern graphics APIs and console SDKs is limited. What I bring is strong C++, genuine
+        systems depth, a habit of profiling and optimising toward hard targets, and the ability to
+        get productive fast in an unfamiliar codebase, plus real enthusiasm for going deep on
+        engine and animation systems. I mentor naturally and I like collaborating, iterating and
+        experimenting on a feature until it's right.
+
+        On the practical side, the Ghent studio suits me well: I'm Belgian, I studied and worked in
+        Belgium for years, so being on-site is a return home rather than a relocation. I'd welcome
+        the chance to talk about how I could contribute to Larian's engine team.
